  "image_name": "0d6b41555f4f4ce7abf432c3c00f10db.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the infection cycle of a geminivirus inside a plant cell. Virus particles enter the cell carrying circular single-stranded DNA, which is delivered to the nucleus and converted into double-stranded DNA associated with nucleosomes, indicating that the viral genome becomes chromatin-like in the host nucleus. From this double-stranded form, the virus both transcribes mRNA to produce viral proteins and replicates its genome, with proteins such as Rep and REn shown as key replication factors.\n\nThe diagram highlights two replication strategies in an inset: rolling-circle replication and recombination-dependent replication. Newly made single-stranded DNA can then be packaged into virus particles through encapsidation, involving the coat protein (CP). These particles are shown as available for acquisition by the whitefly insect vector, linking intracellular replication to transmission between plants.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes movement within the plant. Viral factors including NSP and MP appear to mediate DNA transport from the nucleus toward plasmodesmata, the channels connecting neighboring plant cells. Overall, the image presents the coordinated processes of nuclear replication, gene expression, particle assembly, cell-to-cell movement, and insect-mediated spread that define the geminivirus life cycle.",
